  "construction": {
   "name": "exact_triangular_prism_simplex_subdivision",
   "proof": "Exact triangular-prism self subdivision\n\nLet P be the canonical unit-height triangular prism with planar vertices v0=(1/sqrt(3),0), v1=(-1/(2sqrt(3)),1/2), v2=(-1/(2sqrt(3)),-1/2). Put u=v1-v0 and v=v2-v0. In the triangle M*P, for all i,j>=0 with i+j<=M-1 place an upright unit triangle centered at (M-1)*v0+i*u+j*v. For i+j<=M-2 place a half-turned unit triangle centered at (M-2)*v0+i*u+j*v. These are the standard two simplex types of the triangular subdivision: their closed union is exactly the M-scaled triangle and their interiors are disjoint. The counts are M(M+1)/2 and M(M-1)/2, totaling M^2.\n\nPlace this whole two-frame subdivision at each z=k-(M-1)/2, k=0,...,M-1. The closed unit-height intervals partition[-M/2,M/2]. Thus M^3 complete canonical prisms tile the M-scaled host exactly. Every owner keeps its barycentric source indices and its upright/half-turn parity. Deleting a named suffix of whole owners gives every smaller count at the same scale without changing the retained geometry.\n\nFor M=4 this supplies counts60,61,62,63,64 at scale4. In particular the64-owner ideal construction is optimal: the host volume is s^3 times the unit-prism volume, so64 disjoint interiors force s^3>=64 and s>=4. The certified native files use outward numerical margin; optimality refers to the exact algebraic construction, not to their slightly larger serialized host scales.\n\nThis is classical triangular subdivision and Cartesian extrusion. PingYou Ltd supplies the finite native assembly and certificate; no invention of the infinite triangular tessellation or simplex subdivision is claimed.\n",
   "M": 4,
   "parent_count": 64,
